1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,000
The temple is packed. The air is 
thick with the scent of burning copal,  

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,560
and your parents are whispering prayers to 
Cihuacoatl, goddess of fertility and childbirth,  

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:14,480
while you do what newborns do best—exist. 
Congrats! You’re alive, which is already  

4
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:19,920
a win in Mesoamerica. But don’t get too 
comfortable. Life here is anything but easy. 

5
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:23,360
Your parents offer crops to thank the 
goddess, praying for your health and  

6
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,760
strength. Trust me, you’ll need it.
Then comes the big moment—your  

7
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:34,320
name. Nezahualcóyotl. "Hungry Coyote." Yeah, 
real original. But don’t take it personally.  

8
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:38,400
Names here follow a strict animal-based 
tradition. You could’ve been "Dancing  

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:44,160
Monkey" or "Descending Eagle." Could’ve been 
worse—imagine being called "Squawking Parrot." 

10
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:49,120
Before you even figure out how to walk straight, 
you’re thrown into a boys-only school. And no,  

11
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:54,480
this isn’t some "learn through play" here—this 
is military training disguised as education.  

12
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:59,360
You’re two years old, barely able to 
hold a stick, but discipline is brutal.  

13
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:04,880
No participation trophies. Slacking off? 
That earns you punishment, not a lecture. 

14
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:10,560
Fast-forward eight years. You’re ten, and 
playtime is over. Welcome to real training,  

15
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:15,360
where the curriculum consists of "Ways to 
Hurt People with Sharp Objects." Knives,  

16
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:20,560
axes, fists—whatever gets the job done. 
It’s not just for fun, either. The world  

17
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:26,080
outside Tlaxcala is hostile, and there’s one name 
that keeps coming up in your father’s stories. 

18
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:30,240
The Aztecs.
And spoiler alert: They’re not your buddies. 

19
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:35,360
Your father calls them monsters who prey on the 
weak. Your priest describes them as creeps who  

20
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:40,560
have some unsettling habits with young girls. 
Either way, the message is clear: The Aztecs  

21
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:45,360
are your enemy, and you better start training 
like your life depends on it—because it does. 

22
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:50,400
The military training is exactly as brutal as 
advertised. Your instructors push you like they  

23
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,760
personally hate you. Running drills until your 
legs feel like they’re on fire? Standard. Holding  

24
00:01:55,760 --> 00:02:02,400
a heavy weapon until your arms go numb? Expected. 
And you do it all for one reason—the Flowery Wars.

25
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:06,640
Sounds poetic, right? Don’t be fooled. 
These battles are brutal, bloody,  

26
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:11,840
and relentless. And the Aztecs? They’re training 
just as hard as you are, waiting for their chance  

27
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:16,640
to snap your bones like dried maize stalks.
Better train harder, kid. You’re gonna need  

28
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:21,440
every ounce of strength just to survive.
Who came up with that name, anyway? Flowery  

29
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:28,000
Wars. Sounds nice, right? Like maybe you and 
the Aztecs exchange bouquets. Yeah… not even  

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,240
close. These wars aren’t about wiping 
each other out. They’re about beating  

31
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:37,200
the absolute crap out of your opponent, dragging 
them back home, and showing off how many captives  

32
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:42,560
you’ve taken—like a warrior leaderboard.
And in this game? The MVP isn’t the guy  

33
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:47,440
with most kills—it’s the guy who brings 
home the most living, breathing prisoners. 

34
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:52,240
For seven years, you’ve lived one brutal crash 
course in "How to Beat an Aztec and Not Get  

35
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:58,160
Sacrificed." And now? The moment is here. The 
War Chief just announced that this weekend,  

36
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:03,840
you’ll be clashing with the Aztecs. Finally. 
Time to put a fist in their smug faces. 

37
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:08,720
But wait—plot twist. Before you go off 
to battle, your parents decide it’s the  

38
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:13,200
perfect time to arrange your marriage. Yeah, 
Nothing like looming death to set the mood for  

39
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:18,320
marriage. Your bride-to-be is the daughter of a 
rich neighbor, which sounds great, but there’s a  

40
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:25,200
catch—you don’t get to marry her yet. First, you 
need to come back victorious. No pressure, right? 

41
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:30,960
Here’s the thing about Mesoamerican civilizations: 
they take religion very seriously. Like,  

42
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:35,520
"If there’s a famine, let’s sacrifice 
a bunch of people to fix it" seriously. 

43
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:42,080
By the 15th century, food shortages were brutal. 
The solution? Mass human sacrifice to satisfy the  

44
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:48,400
gods. The Flowery Wars? Yeah, They’re basically 
a regulated way to stock up on sacrifices.  

45
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:53,200
You’re not just fighting for glory—you’re 
fighting to avoid becoming the next offering. 

46
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:57,680
Finally, the weekend arrives. Your 
heart is pounding, your war paint is on,  

47
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:02,640
and it’s time to prove yourself. But 
let’s be real—you already know the stakes. 

48
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:07,040
Losing doesn’t mean going home in shame. It 
means getting paraded through enemy streets  

49
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:12,480
before getting sacrificed on an Aztec altar. 
Not exactly how you planned to end the week. 

50
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:17,120
So you do what any ambitious warrior would 
do: you fight like hell, capture as many  

51
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:22,000
Aztecs as possible, and come home a hero. 
You’ve got a wedding to attend, after all. 

52
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,640
The War Chief leads you and the Tlaxcalan 
warriors to the battlefield—a vast open  

53
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:33,120
plain. Across the field, you spot them. 
The Aztecs. Smug. Overconfident. Acting  

54
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:38,560
like they already won. The sight alone ignites 
a fire in your veins. You’ve trained for this  

55
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:43,200
moment since you could hold a weapon. Now 
it’s time to teach these warriors a lesson. 

56
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:47,280
But this isn’t just about fighting—it’s 
about capturing. And you know exactly  

57
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:52,480
who to target. The strong. The handsome. 
The ones with the perfect warrior build. 

58
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:55,280
Why? Because in the world of human sacrifice,  

59
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:00,320
the gods have standards. The better your 
"offering," the greater the captor’s glory.

60
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:05,280
With your heart pounding like a war drum, 
you charge. The moment the battle starts,  

61
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:11,920
you’re on fire. Two warriors go down fast—your 
strikes are precise, your movements smooth. Eyes  

62
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:19,440
are on you now. The Tlaxcalans cheer, the Aztecs 
falter. For a moment, you think—yeah, this is it. 

63
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,960
Then—WHAM.
A warrior twice your size crashes  

64
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:29,840
into you like a falling temple wall. Before you 
can react, fists hammer down on you. Hard. Fast.  

65
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:36,640
Ruthless. You fight back, but it’s not enough. 
This guy is bigger, stronger, and he’s not alone.  

66
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:42,240
Your friends try to help, but in the chaos of 
war, they’re powerless to stop what happens next. 

67
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:47,680
You. Have. Been. Captured.
And the worst part? You’re exactly what the  

68
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:54,000
gods want. Strong. Good-looking. Prime sacrifice 
material. All those years of training, all those  

69
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:01,200
endless drills—for what? To be an offering on some 
Aztec altar? You want to rage. You want to scream.  

70
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:06,640
But showing weakness? That’s not an option.
The battle ends in a draw. Both sides capture  

71
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:12,960
enough to satisfy the gods. But you? You’re just 
part of the loot. They march you to Tenochtitlán,  

72
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:18,640
the heart of the Aztec Empire. The city is 
massive, unlike anything you’ve ever seen before.  

73
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:23,440
As you enter, crowds line the streets, 
cheering for the victorious warriors.

74
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:28,880
And your captor? Yeah, he’s the star 
of the show. The Aztecs chant his name,  

75
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:34,080
showering him with praise. He’s their hero. 
The warrior who brought home a prized Tlaxcalan  

76
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:40,000
fighter. And you? You should be the one basking 
in glory. Instead, you’re the next sacrifice.

77
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,000
The destination? Templo Mayor. This place 
isn’t just any temple—it’s the religious  

78
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:51,680
heart of the Aztec world, towering with two 
massive pyramids. One is dedicated to Tlaloc,  

79
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:58,160
the rain god, and the other? Huitzilopochtli—the 
god of war, the one who demands blood. 

80
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:02,400
And guess which god you are here for?
Yeah. You already know. 

81
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:07,680
You’ve heard the stories. You know what happens 
next. But there’s a sliver of hope. Not everyone  

82
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:14,080
will be sacrificed. Some captives get chosen 
as slaves. And that? That’s your chance.

83
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:17,440
A priest stops in front of 
you. You brace yourself. 

84
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:20,960
Then the priest smiles.
"Too handsome for slavery,"  

85
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:26,960
he says. "Your beauty belongs on the altar."
Are you serious? This is how you die? Because  

86
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:31,200
you’re too good-looking? You used to 
take pride in your looks, but right now,  

87
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:36,400
you wish you had been a little uglier.
Once you’re marked for sacrifice, the real rituals  

88
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:42,000
begin. You’re no longer just a captive—you’re 
a chosen one, a sacred offering to the gods. 

89
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,920
That means special treatment: 
Cleansing baths, special food,  

90
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:50,880
a designated room. Almost comforting—if 
it weren’t prepping you for execution. 

91
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:56,480
And just to make things worse? They parade you 
around the city. Yep. Public displays. People  

92
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:01,760
come to see the next batch of victims, pointing 
and whispering. It’s basically a casting call,  

93
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:07,600
except the role you landed is "sacrificial 
offering"—and there’s no encore. One week.  

94
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:12,640
That’s all you have left. And unless something 
changes fast, your story is about to end on top  

95
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:17,840
of a pyramid, with a knife to your chest.
Your final day on Earth is here. No more  

96
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:24,480
waiting. No more hoping. This is it. The priests 
lead you up the towering steps of Templo Mayor,  

97
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:28,960
and despite everything—despite knowing 
exactly why you're here—you can't help but  

98
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:35,600
admire the view. From up here, Tenochtitlán looks 
breathtaking. The temples, the canals, the golden  

99
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:39,520
light of the sun hitting the great city.
But you don’t get much time to enjoy the  

100
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:46,000
scenery. Because before you know it, you’re 
on the altar. Held down. Trapped. Waiting.  

101
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:51,360
Then the priest raises the knife. One 
sharp, swift motion—and it's over. 

102
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:56,320
Your heart is lifted into the sky, still 
beating as an offering to Huitzilopochtli,  

103
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:01,600
the god of war. Your body tumbles down 
the pyramid, step by step, as the crowd  

104
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:09,280
erupts in celebration. To them, you are not a 
person. You are a gift, a sacrifice, a necessity. 

105
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:15,680
You’d think dying would bring some kind of peace, 
but no. Your ghost lingers, forced to watch.  

106
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:21,360
And what you see? It makes your blood—well, 
whatever ghosts have instead of blood—boil. 

107
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:27,120
The warrior who captured you—the same one who 
got all the cheers, the praise, the glory—gets  

108
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:34,480
even more. Your body? The nobles feast. Your 
captor takes his share. Because in Aztec culture,  

109
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:41,200
eating a sacrifice means absorbing the power of 
the gods. Yeah. That means ritual cannibalism. 

110
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:47,200
It’s like unlocking a power-up in a video 
game—except the power-up is you. And as if that  

111
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:54,160
wasn’t enough? Your captor gets wealth, status, 
influence—while you get turned into a divine meal. 

112
00:09:54,160 --> 00:10:00,560
You spent your whole life training to defeat the 
Aztecs. To prove Tlaxcala’s strength. And yet,  

113
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:05,040
here you are—A nameless sacrifice. 
A stepping stone in someone else’s  

114
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:11,280
success story. You have unfinished business.
But you’re dead. At least your sacrifice means  

115
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:16,320
a year of good crops for the Aztecs. 
Not that it makes you feel any better.

